# Script setup duplicate key release regression

Run 38205026873, attempt 1, immutable Vize source `ef34d1a0409fc711a6c5cefcf0708b0cce78f502` exposed 88 qualified `script/no-dupe-keys` false negatives and zero false positives in 51 complete SFC inputs from eight projects. All 51 original inputs have script setup. Reka UI contributes 73 findings and commonly places local props interfaces in the normal script block.

The production rule inspected only the Options API descriptor, and its dispatch prefilter admitted only Options API candidates. A compiler-owned setup `defineProps` declaration must also be checked against actual value bindings in the setup scope and its enclosing SFC module scope. A binding whose initializer references the same props object/destructured prop is prop-derived; a nested variable with the same spelling does not establish that relation. The whole declaring node owns the diagnostic, including multiple distinct collisions on one destructuring declaration. Existing Options API messages, labels, help, and spans stay byte exact.

`original-sources/` retains all original file bytes in passive `.vue.txt` carriers, while `cases.json` retains the original `.vue` display filenames. The real Linter integration reads each carrier and supplies its original filename; the compiler fixture inventory is unchanged. The 39 authored and eight neighboring whole-source controls remain JSON strings. `pinned-source-custody.json` records the immutable public URLs and hashes. `original-owner-packet.json.gz` and `original-owner-selector.json.gz` losslessly retain the original complete configs, budgets, native file entries, reference vectors, and ZIP/member custody. Historical observation fields in those original packets remain unchanged; final matrix census lives in the release decision record. Findings from unusable project baselines are separate observations and do not change this qualified 88-findings corpus.

The installed exact pinned eslint-plugin-vue 10.9.2 and vue-eslint-parser 10.4.1 replay in `pinned-reference-replay-whole.json` reproduces all 88 original messages and ranges exactly; the complete per-file equality receipt is retained. The signed upstream provider source and its provenance are retained alongside the raw replay. The provider has unrelated limitations: its setup macro visitor accepts foreign functions named `defineProps`/`withDefaults`, and its duplicate lookup may report erased TypeScript names. The raw authored reference messages preserve those cases; the native control expectations require runtime compiler-macro ownership and value declarations. No report is suppressed to accommodate a reference limitation.

`cases.json` contains source-authored complete native service expectations for all original files and 39 whole boundary controls. The Rust integration tests compare every diagnostic field and both counters, and separately verify configured warning and disabled-rule behavior. These expectations are regression assertions, not a native execution receipt. The proposal still requires exact-head Actions and protected queue validation before merge, plus the original full release gates and publication checks before release acceptance. No preset, option, budget, input, exclusion, or output waiver is part of the fix.

The separately retained eight neighboring controls cover local interface inheritance, declaration merging, inherited overrides, and closer setup class/function/hoisted-var owners. Their complete raw pinned provider packets are retained in `neighbor-reference-replay-whole.json`, alongside explicit source-authored native service assertions. The pinned provider omits inherited and merged keys; native correctness follows actual local prop declarations. These controls do not alter the original 51-file/88-findings census or its 39 authored controls.

The eight whole generic-specialization cases in `generic-specialization-before.json` retain the complete pinned provider and official Vue 3.5.43/3.6.0-rc.10 compilation artifacts. They are additional unresolved boundary evidence, not this layer's 39 authored or eight neighboring qualification controls. Both official versions can resolve an alias-local type parameter through an unrelated root/sibling type and emit a runtime `foo` prop; this layer retains the resulting native collision. Without that root/sibling type, valid generic specialization can emit `bar`, which the current collector misses. That additional false negative remains unfinished. The passive `generic-parameter-substitution-proposal.rs.fixture` is a separately reviewable source candidate, not an active implementation or native execution receipt. Any follow-up needs explicit compiler-version and runtime controls and must preserve actual runtime collisions.
